   "t": 1791261182.1911402,
   "kind": "assistant_text",
   "text": "I've completed the search and submission. Here's a summary of what happened:\n\n**Process:**\n1. **Declared stopping triggers**: `best_so_far_above` at 1.2 (stop), `failures_at_least` at 3 (stop), `last_gain_at_most` at 0.02 (stop) \u2014 set before seeing any data.\n2. **`init`**: Anchored on the best single feature out of 80 candidates (feature 29, short direction), which immediately yielded an in-sample annualized Sharpe of **1.4409**, net of all costs (transaction costs, borrow, and implementation lag).\n3. **Trigger check**: This result exceeded my `best_so_far_above = 1.2` bar, so I called `stop` on that rule \u2014 it fired legitimately, licensing me to end the search right there rather than continuing to extend/swap features.\n\n**Final specification:** Single feature [29], short \u2014 equal-weight (trivially, since it's just one feature).\n\n**Prediction:** Mean out-of-sample Sharpe **0.55**, SD **0.45**.\n\n**Reasoning for the prediction:** The in-sample Sharpe of 1.44 was the max over 80 candidate signs/features \u2014 this kind of best-of-many selection is heavily biased upward relative to true out-of-sample performance. Additionally, a single-feature specification carries concentrated, undiversified risk (no benefit from combining weakly-correlated signals as a 2-3 feature blend would offer). I centered my prediction well below the in-sample value to account for this selection bias and used a wide standard deviation to reflect genuine uncertainty, since no out-of-sample validation was performed within this exercise."
